least index on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ties), then colour in the reverse of that removal order. At most degeneracy + 1 colours, so","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"at most 6 on a planar graph. O((n + m) log n).","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/smallestLast","type":"topic","title":"ColoringStrategy.smallestLast"},"doc://ColoringModule/s16SendableMetatypeP":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s16SendableMetatypeP","title":"Swift.SendableMetatype","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/connectedSequentialDepthFirst":{"title":"ColoringStrategy.connectedSequentialDepthFirst","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialDepthFirst","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"connectedSequentialDepthFirst"}],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The same in depth-first preorder. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/connectedsequentialdepthfirst","type":"topic"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/connectedSequentialBreadthFirst":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"connectedSequentialBreadthFirst"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/connectedsequentialbreadthfirst","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Components by least vertex, each in breadth-first order from its least vertex, neighbours in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges"},{"type":"text","text":" order. O(n + m)."}],"type":"topic","title":"ColoringStrategy.connectedSequentialBreadthFirst","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialBreadthFirst"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/independentSet":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"independentSet"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/independentset","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Colour classes one at a time: class k is a maximal independent set of the vertices not yet"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"coloured, built by repeatedly taking the one with the fewest neighbours among those still"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"available (the least index on ties) and making it and its neighbours unavailable."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"O(χ (n + m) log n) for χ classes."}],"type":"topic","title":"ColoringStrategy.independentSet","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/independentSet"},"doc://ColoringModule/s8SendableP":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s8SendableP","title":"Swift.Sendable","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/saturationLargestFirst":{"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","title":"ColoringStrategy.saturationLargestFirst","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/saturationLargestFirst","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"DSatur (Brélaz 1979): first a vertex of greatest degree, then repeatedly the uncoloured","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex with the most distinct colours among its neighbours, ties to the greatest degree in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the whole graph, then to the lesser index. Exact on bipartite graphs. O((n + m) log n).","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"saturationLargestFirst"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/saturationlargestfirst"},"doc://ColoringModule/SH":{"type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/SH","title":"Swift.Hashable"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/coloredNeighbors":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"coloredNeighbors","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/coloredneighbors","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"First the vertex of greatest degree (the least index on ties), then repeatedly the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"uncoloured vertex with the most coloured neighbours. Ties among those fall as a two-way","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"indexed binary max-heap breaks them, not by index: vertices are pushed in index order and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"raised neighbour by neighbour in ascending order; an entry moves above an equal parent and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"sinks to the left child unless the right one is strictly greater. O((n + m) log n).","type":"text"}],"title":"ColoringStrategy.coloredNeighbors","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/coloredNeighbors"},"doc://ColoringModule/SQ":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/SQ","title":"Swift.Equatable","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/largestFirst":{"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/largestFirst","title":"ColoringStrategy.largestFirst","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Degree descending, the lesser index first on ties: Welsh–Powell, which colours class by","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"class in this order and gives the same colouring as first fit. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"largestFirst"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/largestfirst"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule","type":"topic","title":"ColoringModule","kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule","role":"collection","abstract":[]},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy","type":"topic","title":"ColoringStrategy","kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ColoringStrategy"}],"abstract":[{"text":"How ","type":"text"},{"code":"greedyColoring(strategy:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" orders the vertices. Each vertex in turn takes the least colour","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"none of its already coloured neighbours has (first fit). Degrees are simple degrees (distinct","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"other neighbours, self-loops left out); ties go to the lesser vertex index, except for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":".coloredNeighbors","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ColoringStrategy"}]}}}